// xUnit Test Audit Report - configuration.
//
// Despite the .json extension this file is loaded as a <script>, matching the sister dashboards,
// so it must stay valid JavaScript. Edit it to retarget the report; no HTML changes needed.
//
// -------- What gets reported --------
// The three settings below belong together: the pipeline and the solution exist only inside the
// named project. Connecting with a different project selected is refused with an explanation
// rather than failing later with "no pipeline matches". Change all three together to retarget,
// or set project to "" to lift the check and choose the project by hand.
//
// project   : TFS team project. Pre-selected on the connect form so Connect is a single click.
// pipeline  : the unit test build definition. "id" wins when set (it survives renames); otherwise
//             "name" is matched exactly, and failing that any definition whose name looks like a
//             unit test pipeline. The report always uses that pipeline's newest completed run
//             that published test results.
// solution  : the .sln to report on. Every project in it that references xunit is included, so
//             this one entry covers the whole web solution.
//             "name" is the file name and is what actually identifies the solution - the report
//             searches the repository for it, so it keeps working if the solution moves.
//             "path" is an optional fast hint: when it resolves, no repository listing is needed.
//             If the hint is wrong the report falls back to the search and tells you to update it.
//             Set both to "" to skip solution reporting and scan fallbackTestProjectPath instead.
// fallbackTestProjectPath : used only when no solution is configured, or none can be found.
//
// -------- Access list --------
// users: []      -> no gate. Anyone who can reach the page and holds a valid PAT may use it.
// users: [ ... ] -> only the listed people may load the report. Everyone else is refused.
//
// Matching is on the TFS display name returned by /_apis/connectionData for the entered PAT,
// compared case-insensitively with whitespace collapsed. Names are obfuscated with the same
// XOR+base64 cipher and key as the sister dashboards, so a name encrypted for those works here
// too - use the "Encrypt Username" tab in the Sprint Requirements Tracker to generate values.
// role is recorded and shown next to the identified user; this report has no tabs, so it does
// not currently restrict anything.
//
// IMPORTANT: the access list is a soft gate, not security. The page is a static file, so anyone
// can read this list, read the cipher key, or bypass the check with browser dev tools. Use it to
// make the audience explicit - never to protect anything sensitive. Real protection comes from
// the PAT: without a valid one, no TFS data can be read.

var APP_SETTINGS = {
    "project": "CasepointARA",

    "pipeline": {
        "id": null,
        "name": "UnitTesting"
    },

    "solution": {
        "name": "Casepoint.Web.Core.sln",
        "path": ""
    },

    "fallbackTestProjectPath": "/code/Web/Casepoint.API.Core.Test",

    "users": []
};
